-- title:
--   A single run is the scaled product of its actual phase history
-- statement:
--   Let $n,r\in\mathbb N$, let $o_1,o_2$ be Boolean oracles on subset indicators of an $n$-element set, and fix a tape $t:\mathbb N\to\{0,1\}$. Let $s$ be an annealing schedule with $L$ phases. Define $H_j$ to be the optional cursor reached after the first $j$ phase bodies, starting with the actual allocated tables, current multipliers all equal to $4$, accumulated product $1$, and tape position zero. Absence records an abort. If $P(a)$ denotes the product in a present cursor, then $$\operatorname{singleRun}(r,o_1,o_2,t,s)=\begin{cases}0,&H_L\text{ is absent},\\2^nP(a),&H_L=a\text{ is present}.\end{cases}$$ Here $2^n$ is the value computed by the program’s natural-power routine. This connects the output to prefixes of the same executed phase loop.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/BoundedRunPhaseHistory.lean#L59-L81

